-- title:
--   Genuine Cartier point divisors on every base change of the actual order-13 curve
-- statement:
--   Let K be any field of characteristic zero and C the specified two-chart curve y² = x⁶ + 2x⁵ + x⁴ + 2x³ + 6x² + 4x + 1 with the reciprocal gluing z = x⁻¹, w = yx⁻³. For every K-scheme T and every K-morphism x : T → C, the graph of x in C ×_K T is an effective Cartier divisor: its genuine scheme-theoretic ideal is locally generated by a non-zero-divisor. The proof derives smooth relative dimension one and separatedness from this actual curve. This provides the actual point, diagonal, and constant-basepoint divisor ideals needed for the point-difference Abel–Jacobi line-bundle family. No abstract curve interface, Jacobian witness, Picard representability, or rational-point exclusion is supplied as a hypothesis.
-- source:
--   Explicit sextic and gluing by Vasily Ilin, Apache-2.0: https://github.com/Vilin97/MazurTheorem/tree/54d43d8dda8a6fcf069cc02a815f850d762c5c0c/MazurTorsion/AlgebraicGeometry . Complete official FLT function-field, principal-divisor, Riemann–Roch and Cech/global comparison proofs, Apache-2.0: https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/tree/6e837e75355538c7f80bab5b956861e86c4eacc2 . Sources include S_AlgebraicCurve_RationalFunctionField_stichtenothGenusExists.lean, S_AlgebraicCurve_stichtenothGenusExists_of_isCurveOver.lean, S_AlgebraicCurve_isCurveOver_of_isIntegral_of_smoothOfRelativeDimension_one.lean, S_AlgebraicCurve_cechH1ToH1_bijective.lean and the original Repartitions/AdelicIndex definitions. Actual curve assembly, dimension computation and genus bridge by Vas and contributors. The characteristic-zero strengthening constructs the literal section (0,1), proves its center is a closed point, and applies the complete original S_AlgebraicCurve_Place_isRational_of_range_stalk_section_eq.lean proof. Reuses the complete original Apache-2.0 FLT proof S_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_Hom_isInvertible_ker_of_comp_eq_id.lean and exact original Cartier ideal definitions. The actual smooth base-change and section bridge is derived by Vas and contributors.

